  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Trinh is about to walk up a flight of 9 stairs. She can take either one or two stairs at a time.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p13-q14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "How many different ways can she walk up the flight of stairs?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "55.",
          "worked": "With $b$ two-stair moves, there are $9-2b$ one-stair moves. These $9-b$ moves can be ordered in $\\dfrac{(9-b)!}{b!(9-2b)!}$ ways.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}N&=\\sum_{b=0}^{4}\\frac{(9-b)!}{b!(9-2b)!}\\\\N&=1+8+21+20+5\\\\N&=55.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 45
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "What if she can take either 1, 2 or 3 stairs at a time?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "149.\n\nCount the arrangements separately for 0, 1, 2 or 3 three-stair moves.",
          "worked": "Let $a,b,c$ be the numbers of one-, two- and three-stair moves. They satisfy $a+2b+3c=9$. Each feasible case has $\\dfrac{(a+b+c)!}{a!b!c!}$ arrangements.\n\nFor $c=0$, part (a) gives 55 arrangements. For the remaining cases:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}N_{c=1}&=\\frac{7!}{6!1!}+\\frac{6!}{4!1!1!}+\\frac{5!}{2!2!1!}+\\frac{4!}{3!1!}\\\\N_{c=1}&=7+30+30+4=71\\\\N_{c=2}&=\\frac{5!}{3!2!}+\\frac{4!}{1!1!2!}\\\\N_{c=2}&=10+12=22\\\\N_{c=3}&=\\frac{3!}{3!}=1\\\\N&=55+71+22+1\\\\N&=149.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      }
    ]
  },
